{title: The Water Is Wide}
{artist: Traditional (Scottish, collected by Cecil Sharp)}
{key: G}
{license: public-domain}
{tempo: 76}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]The water is [C]wide, I [G]cannot get [D]o'er
[C]Neither have [G]I wings to [D]fly
[G]Give me a [Em]boat that will [C]carry [D]two
[G]And both shall [C]row, my [G]love and [D]I
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[G]A ship there [C]is and she [G]sails the [D]sea
[C]She's loaded [G]deep as deep can [D]be
[G]But not so [Em]deep as the [C]love I'm [D]in
[G]I know not [C]if I [G]sink or [D]swim
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[G]Oh love is [C]handsome and [G]love is [D]fine
[C]And love's a [G]jewel while it is [D]new
[G]But when it is [Em]old it [C]groweth [D]cold
[G]And fades a[C]way like [G]morning [D]dew
{end_of_verse}
